From: OpenWrt packaging fix
Subject: nmux.h: add missing <sys/select.h>

nmux.cpp uses fd_set/FD_ZERO/FD_SET/FD_ISSET/select() (POSIX,
declared by <sys/select.h>) but nmux.h never includes that header.

This built anyway under glibc, because glibc's own <sys/socket.h>
happens to transitively drag in the fd_set/select declarations as an
implementation detail. musl's <sys/socket.h> does not do this
leakage - it only declares what POSIX actually assigns to that
header - so on OpenWrt's musl toolchain g++ fails with a wall of
"'fd_set'/'select'/'FD_ZERO'/'FD_SET'/'FD_ISSET' was not declared in
this scope".

Reproduced verbatim against real musl headers (musl-gcc/g++ -isystem
musl's include dir) with nmux.h's exact pre-fix include list -
identical error set and identical "did you mean 'L_SET'" typo
suggestion for FD_SET as in the real OpenWrt build log. Adding the
missing standard header resolves it cleanly on musl and is a no-op
everywhere else (glibc/uClibc), since it's simply the header that's
supposed to provide these symbols per POSIX in the first place.

--- a/nmux.h
+++ b/nmux.h
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 #include <arpa/inet.h>
 #include <sys/socket.h>
 #include <netinet/in.h>
+#include <sys/select.h>
 #include "tsmpool.h"
 
 #define MSG_START "nmux: "
